<section aria-labelledby="faq-h" style="border-top: 1px solid #111111">
<div style="max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 88px 24px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 40px">
<h2 id="faq-h" style="flex: 1 1 280px; margin: 0; font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px); line-height: 1.05; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.04em">FAQ</h2>
<div style="flex: 2 1 560px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; border-top: 1px solid #111111">
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">Does it replace ssh?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">For interactive work, yes. You need ssh once, to enroll; after that osewa runs on its own port (TCP and UDP 7322).</p></details>
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">What if UDP is blocked?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">The client races QUIC against a TLS WebSocket and takes whichever connects first. With UDP blocked, that costs 300 ms per connect.</p></details>
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">How is it different from mosh?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">mosh keeps sessions alive and predicts echo too, but it is UDP-only and carries only the terminal. osewa adds TCP fallback, port forwarding and exec, and keeps keystrokes ahead of bulk data on the same connection.</p></details>
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">Does it work behind a VPN or tailnet?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">Yes. QUIC starts with 1200-byte packets to fit a WireGuard MTU, and our Mac-to-Mac runs to Tokyo, in the README, were taken over Tailscale.</p></details>
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">Is it as safe as ssh?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">The cryptography is: TLS 1.3 only, and both ends prove who they are with Ed25519 keys on every connection, no passwords. The server&#x27;s key comes to you during enrollment over ssh, so there&#x27;s no &ldquo;trust this host?&rdquo; prompt, and a revoked device is out on its next connect. Where ssh is ahead is age: OpenSSH has decades of audits and osewa has none yet, it adds a second open port, and its device key is a plain file (mode 0600), not one behind a passphrase or an agent.</p></details>
<details style="border-bottom: 1px solid #D3D3CE; padding: 18px 0"><summary style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 500; min-height: 28px">Can a program on the host reach into my terminal?</summary><p style="margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #4A4A46">Only through what osewa lets past. The screen is parsed on the host, and only known sequences reach your terminal: other OSCs, DCS (including tmux passthrough), APC, PM and SOS are dropped, and titles lose their control characters. Clipboard reads go only to the session&#x27;s owner, and only when they attached with <code style="font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px">--clipboard-read</code>.</p></details>
